= Tangerine Peel (陳皮)

_A single plant —_ Citrus reticulata _Blanco — treated by classical Chinese medicine as_ multiple distinct drug-forms _by part-of-fruit and by age-of-peel; the substance-by-saṃskāra paradigm of the ginger and rehmannia entries applies. The principal drug is_ 陳皮 (chénpí, "aged peel") _— the_ aged dried peel of the ripe mandarin orange, _with the aging itself a constitutive_ saṃskāra _of the substance: the longer the aging, the more prized the drug. Status: Chinese — fully canonical from the_ Shennong Bencao Jing _onward; among the most-used drugs of the entire Chinese pharmacopoeia. Ayurveda: not species-specific classical (the Chinese mandarin reached India late) — reasoned analysis on the general_ nāraṅga / citrus _axis. Tibb: not species-specific in medieval Tibb (the Chinese mandarin reached the Mediterranean world post-Avicenna); the broader_ Citrus _genus is canonical under utrujj/nāranj — see Tibb section. Greco-Roman: Citrus medica (citron) only — the mandarin was unknown to the classical Mediterranean._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Citrus reticulata_ Blanco, family Rutaceae — the *mandarin orange* / *tangerine*; older synonyms _C. nobilis_ Lour., _C. deliciosa_ Ten. The drug-foodstuff is the *dried peel* (exocarp + albedo) of the ripe fruit. _Citrus_ is from Latin _citrus_, originally referring to the citron (_C. medica_, the only Citrus known to the classical Mediterranean) — the genus name was extended to all citrus in the post-Linnaean botanical reform. _Reticulata_ = "netted, reticulated" — referring to the loose skin that pulls easily away from the fruit, the defining mandarin character.#footnote[Etymology and species: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _citrus_); the classical Latin _citrus_ referred to the citron tree only; the genus-name extension is Linnaean. Mandarin reached Mediterranean Europe only in the early 19th c.; the species is Chinese-origin. Standard botanical authority for _Citrus_ taxonomy is contested (Tanaka system vs. Swingle system); _C. reticulata_ is one of the three "true" Citrus species per modern phylogenetics (with _C. maxima_ pomelo and _C. medica_ citron).]

*English:* _Tangerine_ — from French _tangerine_ ("of Tangier"), referring to the Tangier port through which the fruit reached European markets from North African cultivation in the 19th c. _Mandarin_ — from the Mandarin Chinese language association, the small fruit (and the small civil-servant officials called "mandarins" wearing similar bright orange robes); enters English via Spanish-Portuguese _mandarim_. In Chinese tradition the two are not distinguished. _Tangerine peel_, _Mandarin peel_, and _Chen Pi_ are all in modern English pharmacy use; *Chen Pi* (often capitalized) is the standard term in modern Western herbal use.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "tangerine", "mandarin", "Chen Pi"). The Tangier-port etymology of "tangerine" is the standard.]

*Chinese — the canonical drug-form distinctions:*
- *陳皮 (chénpí, "aged peel")* — the *principal drug*; aged dried peel of the ripe mandarin, with the *aging itself the constitutive saṃskāra*. Classical doctrine: peel of less than 1 year is "new peel" (新皮 xīnpí) and is inferior; the *3+ year aged peel* is canonical; *10-year aged* is premium; *30-year aged Xinhui chen pi* is collector-grade and commands extraordinary prices. The aging process — slow oxidation, microbial transformation, volatile reduction, and partial Maillard browning under controlled humid storage — is *real saṃskāra-class chemistry*.
- *青皮 (qīngpí, "green peel")* — *immature unripe-fruit peel*; more pungent and dispersive than chénpí; classically a separate drug. Treated as a Liver-qi-coursing drug with stronger qi-moving action.
- *橘紅 (júhóng, "tangerine red")* — the *outer red-orange layer* of the peel only (flavedo), with the white albedo removed; particularly used for pulmonary applications.
- *橘白 (júbái, "tangerine white")* — the *inner white layer* (albedo) only, with the red layer removed; used for digestive-strengthening applications.
- *橘絡 (júluò, "tangerine net")* — the *fibrous strands* (vascular bundles) between segments; a separate drug for chest-and-collateral pain.
- *橘核 (júhé, "tangerine seed")* — the *seeds*; a separate drug for Liver-channel cold-pattern hernia.
- *橘葉 (júyè, "tangerine leaf")* — the *leaves*; another separate drug for Liver-qi stagnation with breast distension.

The classical _Bencao Gangmu_ (1578, Li Shizhen) treats these as *seven distinct drugs from one plant* — among the most-elaborated single-plant differentiations in the Chinese pharmacopoeia.#footnote[The seven drug-forms from _Citrus reticulata_: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, fruit section; systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004), with separate entries for the principal forms.]

*Xinhui chen pi (新會陳皮):* the *single most-prized regional origin* — Xinhui district in Guangdong, where the *Da Hong Gan (大紅柑)* mandarin cultivar grown on the brackish-water-influenced river-delta soil yields peel with distinctive volatile-oil and flavonoid profile. The *geographic indication (GI) protection* on Xinhui chen pi is real (Chinese national PDO-equivalent designation), and the regional aging tradition is centuries-old.

*Sanskrit:* no species-specific classical name — _Citrus reticulata_ reached South Asia after the saṃhitā period. The broader Citrus genus is treated under:
- *नारङ्ग (_nāraṅga_)* — generic citrus, principally the sour-orange / bitter-orange; the Sanskrit-Persian-Arabic chain naranj → naranja → "orange" is the global word-family origin (the English "orange" word descends ultimately from Sanskrit _nāraṅga_).
- *बीजपूर (_bījapūra_)* — citron (_C. medica_), the only Citrus species known to the classical Mediterranean and South-Asian world.
- *मातुलुङ्ग (_mātuluṅga_)* — another citron / lemon-class term.
The mandarin enters South-Asian usage as नारंगी (_nāraṅgī_) in modern Hindi or as a transliteration. No saṃhitā-canonical entry for _C. reticulata_ specifically.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _nāraṅga_, _bījapūra_, _mātuluṅga_); the global "orange" word-family from Sanskrit _nāraṅga_ via Persian _nārang_, Arabic _nāranj_, Spanish _naranja_, Old French _orenge_ (with the agglutinated "l'orenge" → "the orange"), English _orange_ — one of the cleanest cross-cultural fruit-name transmissions.]

*Arabic / Persian:* the broader Citrus genus is canonical in Tibb. *يوسف أفندي (yūsuf efendī)* — Egyptian Arabic for mandarin / tangerine specifically, named after the Yusuf Pasha who introduced the cultivar to Egypt in the 19th c. *قمري (qamarī)* — Levantine usage. *کیلینو (klymantīn / klyminteen)* — modern transliteration of "clementine" (a tangerine cultivar). *Nāranj* / *burtuqāl* — sour-orange and sweet-orange respectively, both pre-mandarin Arabic. The mandarin / tangerine is *post-Avicennan* in the Mediterranean world, reaching the Levant only in the 19th c.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

陳皮 is among the most-used drugs of the entire Chinese pharmacopoeia, central to the *理氣 (regulate qi)* and *化痰 (transform phlegm)* categories.#footnote[陳皮 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_; full entry in _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; systematized in Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. — 陳皮 appears as the chief or significant supporting drug in dozens of foundational formulas.]

*陳皮 (Aged tangerine peel) — the principal drug:*

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Warm (溫).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Pungent (辛), bitter (苦), aromatic (芳香).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺).

*Actions & indications:*
- 理氣健脾 — regulates qi and strengthens the Spleen; the cardinal action — for qi-stagnation in the middle burner with abdominal distension, fullness, and poor appetite.
- 燥濕化痰 — dries damp and transforms phlegm; for damp-phlegm cough with sticky white phlegm, chest oppression, nausea from damp-stagnation.
- 降逆止嘔 — downbears counterflow and stops vomiting; for nausea, hiccup, vomiting (from cold-damp or qi-stagnation pattern).
- 和中 — harmonizes the middle; supporting role.
- *Harmonizing of other drugs* — like licorice, 陳皮 is among the major *adjuvant / harmonizing* drugs in Chinese formula composition; it moderates tonifying drugs (preventing the cloying-stagnation tendency) and supports digestive drugs.

The *aged-peel saṃskāra logic*: fresh tangerine peel is *too pungent and dispersive*; aging transforms the volatile-oil profile (reducing the harshly-pungent limonene and increasing the aromatic-mellow oxygenated derivatives) and *converges the medicine* toward the canonical Spleen-Stomach-Lung indication. The classical phrase _陳皮以陳為貴_ ("chen pi is prized for being aged") is universal in Chinese pharmacy.

*Cautions:* yin-vacuity with dry cough; heat-pattern phlegm (yellow sticky phlegm with fever); excessive bleeding patterns; pregnancy at high dose (the dispersive action).#footnote[Action list and cautions: _Bencao Gangmu_ 陳皮 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The aging-saṃskāra doctrine is universal in Chinese pharmacy and is one of the cleanest cases of process-as-substance.]

*青皮 (Green tangerine peel) — the unripe-peel drug:*

*Property:* Warm. *Flavor:* Pungent, bitter (more strongly than chénpí). *Channels:* Liver (肝), Gallbladder (膽), Stomach (胃).

*Actions:* 疏肝破氣 — soothes the Liver and breaks up qi-stagnation (much stronger qi-moving than chénpí); 消積化滯 — disperses food accumulation and stagnation; topical for breast nodules and hernias.

*Cautions:* qi-vacuity; pregnancy (the strong breaking-qi action is contraindicated).

*橘紅 (Tangerine red, flavedo only):*

*Property:* Warm. *Flavor:* Pungent, bitter, aromatic. *Channels:* Lung, Spleen.

*Actions:* 散寒燥濕 (disperses cold, dries damp); 化痰利氣 (transforms phlegm and regulates qi) — *particularly indicated for Lung phlegm* (the white-bark Spleen indication of chénpí, but with stronger emphasis on the Lung).

*橘白 (Tangerine white, albedo only):*

*Property:* Warm. *Flavor:* Bitter, slightly sweet. *Channels:* Spleen, Stomach.

*Actions:* 和胃化濕 (harmonizes the Stomach and resolves damp) — the gentler half of chénpí's action; for Spleen-Stomach support without strong dispersion.

*橘絡, 橘核, 橘葉* — separate drugs; see Bensky for full treatment.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No species-specific classical Sanskrit entry for_ Citrus reticulata. _The placement below is reasoned analysis on the general nāraṅga / citrus axis. The broader Citrus genus is in the saṃhitā tradition via_ nāraṅga / bījapūra _but the mandarin specifically is post-classical._

*Reasoned rasa:* Amla (अम्ल, sour — the principal taste of the fresh peel), Kaṭu (कटु, pungent), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent). The mandarin-peel multi-rasa profile.

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp), Sūkṣma (सूक्ष्म, subtle — the aromatic property of volatile-oil-rich drugs in Ayurvedic dravyaguna).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, warming) — converges with the Chinese 溫 classification.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta and Kapha (the warming-aromatic-dispersing register); aggravates Pitta in excess (the uṣṇa-amla profile).

*Reasoned karma:* _Dīpana, pācana_ (kindles digestion, supports āma-digestion — the cross-system convergence with Chinese 健脾 / 理氣); _vāta-anulomana_; _kāsa-hara, śvāsa-hara_ (relieves cough and dyspnea — cold-damp pattern); _kapha-hara_; _hṛdya_ (cardiotonic); _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_ (breath-clearing — the aromatic register). The classical _agni-saṃskāra_ via aged-peel preparation parallels the Chinese aging tradition.

The modern South-Asian Unani-Ayurvedic functional-food register treats *chen pi tea* as a digestive aromatic; the reasoning is consistent with the classical Citrus principles.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S, N, F, M)_

- *(N) Conditional:* Pitta excess; heavy heat-pattern conditions.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Drug interactions*: like grapefruit and other citrus peels, tangerine peel contains *furanocoumarins* (bergamottin and related) that *inhibit CYP3A4*, increasing the plasma concentration of many co-administered drugs (statins, calcium-channel blockers, immunosuppressants). The effect is *less than grapefruit's* but real; clinically significant for very heavy long-term consumption.
  - *Anticoagulant interaction*: theoretical bleeding-time effect via flavonoid antiplatelet activity.
  - *Citrus allergy / sensitization*: cross-reactivity with other Rutaceae.
  - *Pregnancy*: 青皮 (green peel) specifically contraindicated; 陳皮 generally considered safe in culinary doses.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Citrus peels are canonical in classical Tibb under the broader Citrus genus, with the_ utrujj _(citron) being the principal Avicennan-attested form. The_ tangerine / mandarin _specifically is post-Avicennan — reaching the Mediterranean world only in the 19th c. — but is fully integrated in modern Persian, Arabic, and South-Asian Unani as one of the Citrus-peel class. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* قشر يوسف أفندي (_qishr yūsuf efendī_, "Yusuf Efendi peel"); قشر القمري (_qishr al-qamarī_) — Levantine; قشر الكلمنتين / قشر اللآلوس (_qishr al-kelementīn_, _qishr al-yūsufī_); the broader Citrus peel category is قشر الأترج (_qishr al-utrujj_, citron peel) and قشر النارنج (_qishr al-nāranj_, sour-orange peel) — both canonical Avicennan.

Persian: پوست یوسف افندی (_pust-e yusuf efendī_), پوست نارنگی (_pust-e nārangī_).

Urdu/Hakeemi: پوست سنترہ (_post-e santra_), with مالٹا (_māltā_, sweet-orange) as a related substance.

*Mizāj:* by extension from the canonical Citrus-peel class — *Hot and Dry in the 1st–2nd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _qishr al-utrujj_ and _qishr al-nāranj_; modern Unani extension to tangerine peel follows the same classification). Converges with the Chinese 溫 warming and with the reasoned Ayurvedic uṣṇa.

*Af'āl:* *Mufattiḥ li-l-suddad* (deobstruent — for hepatic and gastric stagnation); *muqawwī al-mi'da* (gastric tonic — the canonical citrus-peel action); *kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* (carminative); *muḥsin al-hawḍm* (digestive); *mughayyir li-l-balgham* (phlegm-modifying); *mufariḥ* (mood-elevating — the aromatic register, paralleling jasmine and rose); *jālī* (cleansing); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī* (vital-spirit tonic via aromatic).

*Istemālāt:* dyspepsia; flatulence; cold-pattern cough with phlegm; mood disturbance with digestive component; post-meal digestive support; cold-pattern abdominal pain.

*Mawāne':* hot-pattern conditions in excess; pregnancy at therapeutic dose; gastric ulcer with bleeding.

*Muṣliḥ:* honey; rose-water; sugar.

*Badal:* sour-orange peel (*qishr al-nāranj*); cardamom (*hīl*) for aromatic-digestive function.

*Tibb formulas:* *Sufūf-i Post-e Santra* — tangerine-peel powder for digestive use; *Sharbat-i Yūsufī* — tangerine-peel syrup; *Joshānda-yi Post-e Nārangī*; modern Unani manufacturer products in CCRUM publications. The Egyptian *yūsuf efendī* peel is used in cold-pressed *citrus essential-oil* preparations and in confectionery, with mild medical status.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _qishr al-utrujj_ and _qishr al-nāranj_ — the canonical Citrus-peel framework; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _post-e santra_ entry — modern South-Asian Unani standard; the mandarin-specific entry is post-classical.#footnote[Tibb tangerine peel: the canonical Citrus-peel framework is Avicennan; the species-specific mandarin / tangerine entry is post-classical, reflecting the 19th-c. arrival of the species in the Mediterranean world.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

陳皮 appears in a substantial fraction of all classical Chinese formulas — representative examples:

- *二陳湯 (Èr Chén Tāng, "Two-Aged Decoction")* — *the foundational damp-phlegm formula*; 陳皮 + 半夏 (pinellia) + 茯苓 (poria) + 甘草 (licorice) + 生薑 (fresh ginger) + 烏梅 (mume). Named for the *two aged drugs* — 陳皮 and 半夏 — which work best when aged. The basis of countless derivative formulas.
- *平胃散 (Píng Wèi Sǎn, "Calm the Stomach Powder")* — 蒼朮 (atractylodes) + 厚朴 (magnolia bark) + 陳皮 + 甘草; for damp-stagnation in the middle burner with abdominal fullness and bloating.
- *六君子湯 (Liù Jūn Zǐ Tāng, "Six Gentlemen Decoction")* — 人參 + 白朮 + 茯苓 + 甘草 + 陳皮 + 半夏; for Spleen-qi vacuity with damp.
- *香砂六君子湯 (Xiāng Shā Liù Jūn Zǐ Tāng)* — six gentlemen plus 木香 + 砂仁; for Spleen-qi vacuity with qi-stagnation.
- *參苓白朮散 (Shēn Líng Bái Zhú Sǎn)* — 陳皮 as supporting ingredient; for Spleen-vacuity diarrhea.
- *補中益氣湯 (Bǔ Zhōng Yì Qì Tāng, "Tonify the Middle and Augment Qi Decoction")* — 陳皮 as the qi-moving counterweight to the heavy tonifying drugs.
- *逍遙散 (Xiāo Yáo Sǎn, "Free and Easy Powder")* — 陳皮 as supporting ingredient (in some variants); for Liver-qi stagnation with Spleen vacuity.

Typical dose: 3–10 g in decoction; smaller for tea / culinary use. Aged 陳皮 (3+ years) is the standard for medicinal use.#footnote[Formula sources: Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009). 二陳湯 from _Tàipíng Huìmín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (太平惠民和劑局方); 六君子湯 from same source; 平胃散 from _Hé Jì Jú Fāng_; 補中益氣湯 from Li Dongyuan's _Pí Wèi Lùn_.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical Sanskrit formulas. Modern Indian functional-food and Hakeemi adoption (*chen pi tea*) is post-classical.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

The mandarin / tangerine reached Mediterranean Europe in the early 19th c. (the cultivar arrived in Sicily via the Tangier port, hence "tangerine"; reached England in the 1820s); the species was *unknown to the classical Greco-Roman world*. The broader Citrus tradition is Mediterranean classical — see _C. medica_ (citron) in Dioscorides and Pliny; sour orange via medieval Arabic transmission; sweet orange via Portuguese-Asian trade in the 15th–16th c. The tangerine-peel-as-medicine framing in modern Western herbal practice is a 20th-c. adoption from Chinese medicine; *Western pharmacy* primarily uses *bitter orange peel* (_Citrus aurantium_, _Aurantii pericarpium_) for the same digestive register, which is the European pharmacopoeial citrus drug.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic) medicine

See the substantive section above. The principal preparations are *Sufūf-i Post-e Santra*, *Sharbat-i Yūsufī*, and the broader Citrus-peel class formulas (*Sharbat-i Bazūrī* uses citrus peels among the aromatic-diuretic ingredients).

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Tangerine peel is principally a *subtropical-humid southern Chinese* substance, with cultivation, aging, and consumption concentrated in the Guangdong-Guangxi-Fujian region; the broader Citrus genus has Mediterranean and South-Asian extensions. See _climates.typ_ for the canonical climate framework.

*Subtropical-humid (Guangdong, especially Xinhui 新會 — the canonical chen-pi region).* The Xinhui delta climate combines hot wet summers and mild damp winters with brackish-water river-delta soil; this is the world's premium chen-pi production region. In Ayurvedic _ṛtucaryā_ terms (Caraka Sūtra 6) the year is dominated by extended _varṣā_ (monsoon) and _śarad_ load on Pitta-Kapha with mild winter relief; in 中醫 terms (_Su Wen_ ch. 12, 異法方宜論) the southeastern register is 濕熱 (damp-heat) on the Spleen and Heart; in Tibb terms the climate is *Hot and Moist*, loading on *balgham* (phlegm) and on the stomach. *The traditional response is exactly the chen-pi register*: warming-aromatic-drying-dispersing the damp-stagnation that this climate predictably generates. The *aging tradition* is climate-coupled: the Xinhui aging warehouses use the climate's annual humidity-temperature cycle to drive the slow saṃskāra transformation; the peel is *opened to the air* during dry-cool winters and *closed* during humid summers, the seasonal cycle producing the years-long transformation. *Cares:* the aged peel is brewed in *boiling water* (in contrast to green tea's lower temperatures), in small concentrated cups (gong-fu cha tradition extends to chen-pi tea); paired with *light Cantonese soup* and *dim sum*; the canonical Cantonese *snake-soup* and *winter-warming herbal soup* tradition uses chen pi as a foundational aromatic.

*Mediterranean (broader Citrus, not mandarin-specific).* The Mediterranean climate is *warm-dry summer / cool-wet winter* — supporting the broader Citrus cultivation but not the mandarin specifically (which reached the region only in the 19th c.). For the Mediterranean Citrus tradition, see the bitter-orange-peel European pharmacopoeial usage and the citron-peel classical tradition.

*Modern transposition (deśa-viruddha, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Tangerine peel exported globally as *chen pi tea* and as *traditional Chinese medicine herb* reaches cool-damp Atlantic European, continental-extreme, and cold-dry North American contexts. The warming-aromatic-damp-dispersing register is actually *well-aligned* with cool-damp climates (where the constitutional load is Vāta-Kapha 寒濕 on the Spleen) — chen pi is one of the few Asian herbs whose climate-of-origin pharmacology *translates well* to non-tropical climates because both climate-types feature damp-Spleen issues. The exception is hot-dry climates where the warming character may be inappropriate. *Modern globalized "chen pi spice" use in baking and Asian-fusion cooking* in any climate is climatically benign as long as the dose is culinary. The aged-Xinhui premium-collector market is a separate phenomenon — collector substance, not constitutional medicine — and should be honestly framed as such.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents:*
- *Volatile oil (1–3% of dried peel)* — dominantly *d-limonene* (~50–80% in fresh peel; *decreases substantially with aging*), *γ-terpinene*, *β-myrcene*, *α-pinene*, *linalool*; in aged peel, the limonene proportion drops and oxygenated derivatives rise.
- *Flavanones* — *hesperidin*, *naringin*, *narirutin* — the principal medicinal-claim compounds.
- *Polymethoxyflavones (PMFs)* — *nobiletin*, *tangeretin*, *sinensetin* — distinctive of mandarin peel; *increase with aging*; the basis of modern anticancer / anti-inflammatory research interest.
- *Pectin* — substantial in albedo (橘白); the basis of food-industry pectin extraction.
- *Furanocoumarins* — bergamottin and related; the CYP3A4 inhibitor class, lower than grapefruit but real.
- *Synephrine* — present in trace; the Citrus-genus alpha-adrenergic compound (much higher in bitter orange).
- *Vitamin C* — modest in dried peel; substantial in fresh.

*The aging chemistry — biochemically vindicated:* the classical "aged is better" doctrine has clear chemical correlates — d-limonene oxidation reduces the harshly-pungent volatile profile; polymethoxyflavone (nobiletin, tangeretin) content *increases* in well-aged peel; flavonoid glycoside hydrolysis (to more bioavailable aglycones) progresses with aging; the resulting peel has *different chemistry* from the fresh peel, with greater concentration of the modern-research-relevant active compounds.#footnote[Phytochemistry: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); reviews in _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_, _Food Chemistry_ on Citrus peel chemistry. The aged-vs-fresh chemistry differential is well-documented in published surveys of Xinhui chen pi specifically.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Carminative / antispasmodic:* d-limonene and the volatile oil have documented antispasmodic activity on intestinal smooth muscle; the modern correlate of the classical 理氣 action. Clinical evidence in functional dyspepsia.
- *Antitussive / expectorant:* the volatile oil and PMFs; the modern correlate of the classical 化痰 action.
- *Anti-inflammatory:* nobiletin and tangeretin have substantial preclinical anti-inflammatory activity (NF-κB inhibition, COX inhibition).
- *Antioxidant:* standard flavonoid antioxidant profile.
- *Anti-cancer (preclinical research):* nobiletin and related PMFs have substantial anti-proliferative activity in cell-line studies; clinical translation is preliminary; treat as *research compounds, not clinical drugs*.
- *Hypolipidemic:* small clinical evidence for modest cholesterol-lowering with regular chen pi tea consumption.
- *CYP3A4 inhibition:* the drug-interaction concern; clinically significant for very heavy long-term consumption with critical-dose medications.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the classical Chinese 理氣健脾 + 化痰 + the reasoned Ayurvedic dīpana-pācana + Tibb mufattiḥ + modern carminative-and-PMF pharmacology converges cleanly on the digestive-and-respiratory-supportive register. The aging-saṃskāra doctrine is one of the cleanest classical-modern alignments — biochemistry now vindicates "aged is better" molecule-for-molecule.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary use*: up to ~5 g/day of aged chen pi in cooking and tea is safe and the dietary register.
- *Therapeutic use (TCM)*: 3–10 g/day in decoction; under qualified guidance.
- *Standardized extracts*: typically 500 mg–2 g/day of dried-peel extract.
- *Risk groups (use cautiously)*: (a) concurrent critical-dose drugs metabolized by CYP3A4 (statins, calcium-channel blockers, immunosuppressants); (b) pregnancy at high therapeutic dose; (c) Citrus allergy; (d) bleeding disorders / anticoagulants (theoretical); (e) gastric ulcer with active bleeding.
- *Sourcing*: authentic *Xinhui chen pi* is expensive; commercial market is full of mis-labelled products; for therapeutic use, source from reputable Chinese pharmacy suppliers.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Chinese Pharmacopoeia. Drug-interaction caveat: published herb-drug-interaction literature; the CYP3A4 effect is real but typically clinically modest at culinary doses.]

